INT. PRESBYTERY KITCHEN - 1347 - EARLY MORNING

A low stone room. Smoke blackens the beams. A pot of barley hangs over the fire.

BROTHER AUGUSTINE (50s, tonsured, gentle) stirs. Beside him, SISTER HILDEGARD (40s, wimple, strong forearms) kneads dough on a board. At the table, an old monk, BROTHER BERNARD (70s, blind, hands folded), listens. By the door, a servant boy, ELIAS (12, ragged smock), hugs a bundle of firewood.

AUGUSTINE
The harvest is poor.

HILDEGARD
The harvest is always poor.

BERNARD
It was better when I could see it.

AUGUSTINE
Brother, you could not see it then.

BERNARD ^
I could see it in my mind.

HILDEGARD
That is not seeing.

BERNARD
It is the only kind that lasts.

Elias sets the firewood down near the hearth, careful not to wake the embers. He glances at the pot. Hildegard breaks off a piece of dough, rolls it, and sets it in his hand without looking.

ELIAS
Thank you, Sister.

HILDEGARD
Eat it before the prior sees.

Augustine tastes the barley and nods. Bernard breathes in the smoke and the yeast, the old man's face softening.
